From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Cui <bh@bugs.cc>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 03:44:41 -0400
Subject: feat: share one conversation history across accounts by default

`cswap run` gives every account a profile of its own under
`CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, and the profile's `projects/` (what `claude --resume`
lists) is per account unless the launch carries `--share-history`. The
flag is per launch and there is no setting behind it: forget it once and
`_sync_sharing` unlinks the profile from `~/.claude` again, and the
account starts a second, private history. The same-account fast path
makes it worse. When account N is the default login, `cswap run N` runs
plain `claude` on `~/.claude` and skips the profile entirely, so one
account's sessions land in two places depending on who was the default
at the time. Whoever switches the default and then reaches for `--resume`
finds a conversation missing (realiti4/claude-swap#239), and the profile
of the account that is the default can never be merged, because the code
that merges never runs for it.

A conversation belongs to the user, not to the account that happened to
run it, so sharing is now the default and is persisted:

- `session.shareHistory` (settings.json, `cswap config`) is the durable
  choice, default true. `--share-history` / `--no-share-history` override
  it for one launch; with neither flag `run()` follows the setting. On
  Windows the setting is ignored (history sharing is not available there),
  while an explicit `--share-history` is still refused.
- The same-account fast path syncs the profile before exec'ing plain
  claude, so history the account accumulated while it was not the default
  is merged into `~/.claude` and linked right away, instead of waiting for
  a launch that never comes while the account stays the default.

Together with the previous patch, every launch (`claude`, `cswap run N`
as the default or not) reads and writes one history, and a profile that
forked before the setting existed folds back in on its next launch
without losing a file.

